d\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   53965 Nov 11 08:13 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_081253.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   29423 Nov 11 08:12 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:13 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     280 Nov 11 08:13 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    2915 Nov 11 08:15 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    8353 Nov 11 08:33 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    8379 Nov 11 08:31 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   23248 Nov 11 08:31 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   36329 Nov 11 08:33 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      16 Nov 11 08:12 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:29 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:29 search_plane_metrics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     266 Nov 11 08:17 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     272 Nov 11 08:17 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent       7 Nov 11 08:12 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Provisional title:** How Accurate Is Regression-Based GATE Estimation Under Network Interference? Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Error in an Eight-Case Grid.\n\n## Central question\nIn a single randomized experiment on a network, how accurately can a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which features of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design decide when it works and when it fails?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the eight-case synthetic grid (n=3000; SBM / Barabasi-Albert / Watts-Strogatz; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), regression-GATE error is small (about 3-5% relative RMSE per case) when the fitted exposure mapping matches the mechanism, and large when it does not (saturating 0.198, count x degree 0.151, two-hop 0.452). Graph family, interference strength and cluster vs Bernoulli assignment did not degrade accuracy within the tested range. Nominal 95% intervals undercover (0.839). The frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure regression lowers mean relative RMSE to 0.126 vs 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, but leaves the two-hop case unresolved.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation: completed, outcome `qualified`, 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, 0 missing/failed episodes. No `downstream_study` block, so no downstream claims. Qualification supports reporting the measured results only, not generality or superiority.\n- Rejected: \"new estimator wins\" (only 3 scored variants, same case specs in search and final, no gate ablation, no per-case baselines); search-chronology (diary). Chosen: thesis-first characterization with the frozen estimator as the studied method. Not edit mode, so prior-paper status is \"rejected\" (nothing to preserve).\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure-regression estimator (proposed/studied).\n2. Plain linear exposure regression (baseline, 0.180).\n3. Difference-in-means (0.622) and neighbourhood Horvitz-Thompson (0.994) (baselines).\n4. Correctly specified oracle regression (supporting reference, 0.045).\n5. Search-plane estimator-class alternatives: flexible ridge ensemble (0.232), linear + residual correction (0.185), vs BIC-gated library (0.140) and linear (0.184) on that plane. Ablation-style, labelled search plane.\n6. Negative controls and replay (supporting): constant 9.0e-15, permuted outcome 0.0588, permuted graph RMSE 0.627, 0 vacuity violations, 0 replay diff.\n\n## Results focus\nAggregate method comparison; per-case profile with single-factor contrasts (explicitly case pairs, no per-case intervals); aggregate bias 0.074 vs RMSE 0.126; interval undercoverage (0.839, width 0.379); seed-level variability from the 24 per-seed scores (two outliers, seeds 9001 and 9015); search-plane estimator-class table, kept separate from final.\n\n## Figures (ICLR is single-column; text width about 5.5 in)\n- **Fig. 1 overview_schematic** (generate-concept-figure, full width, page 1-2): pipeline and eight-case factor grid, with the corner-extrapolation vs misspecification error sources.\n- **Fig. 2 main_accuracy** (matplotlib, full width, 2 panels): (a) five methods aggregate relative RMSE, log/broken axis; (b) per-case frozen-estimator error across 8 cases (budget relaxed to 8 categories, 1 series, because the grid has exactly eight cases).\n- **Fig. 3 reliability** (matplotlib, 0.85 width, 2 panels): per-seed scores with bootstrap interval; coverage vs 0.95 nominal.\n- Tables: case-factor table with final per-case error; search-plane estimator-class table.\n- PDF + 300 DPI PNG from one source script; LaTeX references the PDF; no shrinking of dense figures.\n\n## Body-page use (8-9 pages, no padding)\nFraming + Fig. 1 (~1), related work (~0.75), setup/estimator/metric (~1.5), results (~3-3.5), discussion and limitations (~1).\n\n## Supplement\nCase specifications; exact estimator and baseline definitions; per-seed table and bootstrap; negative controls; search-plane comparison; LLM Usage section (required by venue).\n\n## Must-disclose limitations\nSearch and final share case specifications and the library was designed after seeing search-plane per-case errors (no transfer-to-new-mechanism claim); one n, one p; case-pair contrasts, not factorial; no per-case baselines, bias or intervals; two-hop failure cause unknown (gate diagnostics unverified, so unknown); undercoverage cause not established; no public-graph or real-data experiments, no gate ablation (ablation_studies empty), no learned-graph methods.\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: Experiment A/B, search trajectory, iteration history, progression, campaign summary, lessons learned. Phrases: combined_score, correctness rate, eligible, rubric, program_1, \"we first tried\", state-of-the-art claims. Use \"model variant\", \"configuration\", \"seeded run\".\n\n## Writer notes\nCite only relevant bib entries (Chin, Aronow-Samii, Ugander, Karrer, Han-Ugander, Chen et al., Eichhorn et al., Forastiere et al., etc.) after checking provenance; many bib entries are irrelevant noise. Starter-list papers missing from the bib must not be cited without provenance. Anonymize everything. Full machine-readable plan in `paper_strategy.json`.\nfigures:\nmain_accuracy.pdf\nmain_accuracy.png\nmain_accuracy.png.prov.json\noverview_schematic.png\noverview_schematic.prompts.json\nplane_consistency.pdf\nplane_consistency.png\nplane_consistency.png.prov.json\nreliability.pdf\nreliability.png\nreliability.png.prov.json\n\nglobal_memory:\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask\n\nscientific-evidence:\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42579b58-83b8-4626-90f8-5debb8e4f391","uuid":"5ed349d6-71d1-40cb-8a57-53f72c348929","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:33:19.864Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 5552\ndrwx------ 7 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:33 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:12 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent     751 Nov 11 08:12 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent    4096 Nov 11 08:12 .claude\n-rw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:12 .paper_two_stage_enabled\nprw------- 1 agent agent       0 Nov 11 08:33 .postdraft_citation_weave_stream_20261111_081253.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    3685 Nov 11 08:33 VENUE.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    9560 Nov 11 08:29 build_figures.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    7730 Nov 11 08:29 claims_grounding.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    1255 Nov 11 08:29 derived_stats.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent     392 Nov 11 08:29 derived_stats.json.prov.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    3390 Nov 11 08:30 draft_reference_gaps.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    2887 Nov 11 08:30 draft_reference_gaps.md\n-rw------- 