{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PQiGohndr1jUKS3nzhUzXF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Four queries, one or two per gap target in `draft_reference_gaps.json`.\n\n**Central topic:** Error of regression-based GATE estimators under network interference, explained as exposure-mapping misspecification and corner-exposure extrapolation, with confidence intervals that undercover.\n\nThe existing bibliography already covers design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). No queries are spent there. The gap memo flags that area as well cited.\n\n## Queries\n\n1. **Misspecified exposure mapping** (`result_driven_theme`)\n   - Query: *causal inference under misspecified exposure mapping network interference*\n   - Target: `thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification`, introduction, priority high. Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\".\n   - Rationale: Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for exposure mappings. The central thesis needs prior work on what goes wrong when the mapping is wrong.\n\n2. **Unknown interference** (`result_driven_theme`)\n   - Query: *average treatment effects under unknown interference structure*\n   - Target: the same thesis target.\n   - Rationale: This covers unknown or arbitrary interference. `Bhattacharya2019Uncertainty` already handles network uncertainty, so this query aims at estimand and bias results under unknown interference.\n\n3. **Interference background and DiM bias** (`baseline_comparator`)\n   - Query: *causal inference with interference spillover effects difference-in-means bias randomized experiments*\n   - Target: `intro-interference-dim-bias`, introduction, priority medium. Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased for the GATE under interference\".\n   - Rationale: The opening claims about no-interference violations and DiM bias have no foundational citation. Only HT estimators are cited there.\n\n4. **Variance and intervals under network dependence** (`baseline_comparator`)\n   - Query: *variance estimation and confidence intervals under network dependence in interference experiments*\n   - Target: `reliability-interval-undercoverage`, results (reliability subsection), priority medium. Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\".\n   - Rationale: The 0.839 vs 0.95 coverage result is explained by HC1 intervals ignoring dependence. The draft cites no work on network-dependent variance estimation or HAC/cluster-robust inference beyond `Han2023ModelbasedRA`.\n\n## Deliberately excluded\n- Positivity/overlap extrapolation was a search topic in the gap target. It is folded into queries 1 and 2 rather than given its own query, to keep the set small.\n- No GNN/Facebook100 queries, because the gap memo says they appear only as untested extensions in limitations.\n- No retrieval was performed at this stage.\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 4 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/4 | causal inference under misspecified exposure mapping network interference\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 1/25 (24 left) | candidates 1/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 2/25 (23 left) | candidates 2/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#-----------] | refs 3/25 (22 left) | candidates 3/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [##----------] | refs 4/25 (21 left) | candidates 4/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [##----------] | refs 5/25 (20 left) | candidates 5/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [###---------] | refs 6/25 (19 left) | candidates 6/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [###---------] | refs 7/25 (18 left) | candidates 7/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 8/25 (17 left) | candidates 8/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 9/25 (16 left) | candidates 9/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#####-------] | refs 10/25 (15 left) | candidates 10/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#####-------] | refs 11/25 (14 left) | candidates 11/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [######------] | refs 12/25 (13 left) | candidates 12/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [######------] | refs 13/25 (12 left) | candidates 13/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#######-----] | refs 14/25 (11 left) | candidates 14/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [#######-----] | refs 15/25 (10 left) | candidates 15/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 16/25 (9 left) | candidates 16/25 | query 1/4\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 17/25 (8 left) 