--- final message ---
I added four verified references and the prose around them in `paper.tex`. The body ends on page 9, within the 8–9 page budget. `pdftotext` isn't installed here, so I couldn't run the skill's own compile script or `validate_figures.py` (which needs the orchestrator's `src` package). Instead I compiled by hand with `pdflatex` and `bibtex`. It built to 14 pages and the only warnings were two empty-venue warnings for existing entries (Offer-Westort2021DesigningME, Shankar2024ABTU) and `h`→`ht` float-specifier notices. The tex/citation check, claims validation (18 claims, 30 references) and evidence check (0 dangling) all passed. I did not re-run the page-fill check.

**References** (the bibliography stays at the 30-reference cap). I confirmed each new one against OpenAlex records, including DOIs, and recorded them in `references_provenance.json`.

| Added | Placed in | Gap addressed |
|---|---|---|
| Sävje 2023 (Biometrika), misspecified exposure mappings | thesis paragraph, new related-work paragraph | exposure-mapping misspecification |
| Sävje, Aronow & Hudgens 2021 (Annals of Statistics), unknown interference | thesis paragraph, new related-work paragraph | exposure-mapping misspecification |
| Eckles, Karrer & Ugander 2016 (JCI), bias from interference | intro, DiM-bias sentence | DiM bias |
| Li & Wager 2022 (Annals of Statistics), random-graph asymptotics | related work, coverage discussion | variance under network dependence |

To make room I dropped two peripheral entries, `Kipf2016GCN` and `Tiwari2024Quasi`. I also removed their two citation sites: the "e.g., GCN" cite in Limitations and the randomization-test clause in Related Work.

**Text changes.** All edits are limited to the targeted passages:
- **Introduction:** the DiM-bias sentence now cites Eckles and Ugander.
- **Thesis paragraph:** the "exposure-mapping fidelity" claim is now tied to the misspecified/unknown-interference literature.
- **Related work:** there is a new "Misspecified exposure mappings" paragraph. It frames the paper as an empirical simulation measurement, with the corner-extrapolation account kept as a heuristic.
- **Reliability section:** the 0.839 coverage discussion now points to variance and interval work under network dependence and says plainly that none of it was run on this grid.

The new citations carry no numbers, and I described the cited works only at the level their titles support.

The new text pushed the body onto page 10, so I tightened a few sentences in Results and Discussion to bring it back to 9.

I also updated the `claims_grounding.json` citation keys and source ids, and appended entries to `memory.md` and the global memory files.
